    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The Gita is a dialogue between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arjuna">Arjuna</a> and his charioteer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a>, an avatar of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a>, at the start of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurukshetra">Kurukshetra</a> War, and it forms part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahabharata">Mahabharata</a>.</li><li>It brings together Vedic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dharma">dharma</a>, Sankhya-based yoga and knowledge, and bhakti (devotion), and teaches action without craving its fruits.</li><li>Tradition ascribes it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vyasa">Vyasa</a>, while scholars see a composite work, generally dated to the second or first century BCE, which has drawn hundreds of commentaries and readings.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Richard Davis cites a count by Callewaert and Hemraj in 1982 of 1,891 translations of the Bhagavad Gita in 75 languages, 273 of them in English.</p></aside>
    </section>

