    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">2nd or 1st century BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The Gita is generally dated to this time, though earlier and later estimates exist and 200 BCE may be the date of a major revision</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 800</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adi-shankara">Adi Shankara</a> writes the oldest surviving commentary, which reads the text as teaching the non-dualism of Atman and Brahman</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 1100</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramanuja">Ramanuja</a> writes his Gita Bhashya, reading the text as qualified monism and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a>'s birth as physically real</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 1290</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dnyaneshwar">Dnyaneshwar</a> completes the Dnyaneshwari, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marathi">Marathi</a> commentary and the oldest surviving literary work in that language</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1785</span><span class="rf-text">Charles Wilkins publishes the first English translation, with an introduction by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warren-hastings">Warren Hastings</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1910–1911</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bal-gangadhar-tilak">Bal Gangadhar Tilak</a> writes his commentary Shrimadh Bhagavad Gita Rahasya while serving a sentence for sedition</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1946</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Gandhi</a>'s commentary, written in Gujarati in 1929 and translated by Mahadev Desai, is published in English</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">2025</span><span class="rf-text">The Bhagavad Gita <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> is added to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/memory-of-the-world-programme">Memory of the World Register</a></span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/mahabharata"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mahabharata.jpg" alt="Mahabharata" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/mahabharata">Mahabharata</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kurukshetra.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Bhagavad Gita (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a>, literally 'God's song') is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> scripture that forms part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahabharata">Mahabharata</a>, in its sixth book, the Bhishma Parva. It is generally dated to the second or first century BCE, though a later estimate (1st century CE) and earlier ones (400 to 500 BCE) have been given, and 200 BCE may be the date of a major revision. Jeaneane Fowler writes that the dating depends in part on whether the Gita belonged to the early versions of the Mahabharata or was inserted later, and the epic was itself likely compiled over several hundred years.</p><p>Arthur Basham argues that the text belongs to an era when the ethics of war were questioned and renunciation was becoming popular, after the rise of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jainism">Jainism</a> in the 5th century BCE and the life of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashoka">Ashoka</a> in the 3rd, so that a first version may date from the 3rd century BCE or later. Winthrop Sargeant places its language, Epic-Puranic Sanskrit, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panini">Panini</a> but before the long compounds of classical Sanskrit. Kashi Nath Upadhyaya argues instead for the fifth or fourth century BCE.</p><p>Scholars describe it as a synthesis of Vedic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dharma">dharma</a> (duty), Sankhya-based yoga and jnana (knowledge), and bhakti, drawing on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upanishads">Upanishads</a>. Dennis Hudson proposes that the Gita and the Pancaratra Agama share Vasudeva-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a> roots.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="content">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">What It Is</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/krishna"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/krishna.jpg" alt="Krishna" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Krishna_Holds_Up_Mount_Govardhan_(MET_70694)_cropped.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Miskin, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Gita is a dialogue between the Pandava prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arjuna">Arjuna</a> and his charioteer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a>, whom <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> tradition regards as an avatar of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a>, just before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurukshetra">Kurukshetra</a> War. Arjuna asks Krishna to drive to the centre of the battlefield between the two armies. Seeing relatives, friends and teachers among his enemies, he is filled with doubt and despair, drops his bow and wonders whether to renounce his duty. Sanjaya, counsellor of the Kuru king Dhritarashtra, acts as a secondary narrator, with Dhritarashtra as his audience.</p><p>The poem has 18 chapters and 700 verses. In chapter 2 Krishna begins the philosophy, teaching the eternal nature of the soul (atman) and the temporary nature of the body, and advising Arjuna to do his warrior duty with detachment. Chapter 3 treats karma yoga, action without craving its fruits. Chapter 11 shows Arjuna Krishna's universal form (Vishvarupa), chapter 12 praises devotion (bhakti), and chapter 14 explains the three gunas, sattva, rajas and tamas. Chapter 18, the longest at 78 verses, summarises the teaching: renunciation of the fruits of action and surrender to Krishna.</p><p>The text sets out three disciplines, jnana (knowledge), karma (action) and bhakti (devotion). Fowler states that it does not raise any of them to a status that excludes the others; what joins them is 'inner renunciation'. Some scholars treat meditation (raja yoga) as a fourth path.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Bhagavad Gita&#8221; cites where it covers Manuscripts and layout, Structure, Narrative, Chapters and content:</p><ul><li>Keya Maitra, Philosophy of the Bhagavad Gita: A Contemporary Introduction (Bloomsbury Academic, 2018)</li><li>Galvin Flood, The Bhagavad Gita: A New Translation (W.W. Norton &amp; Company, 2013)</li><li>Winthrop Sargeant, The Bhagavad Gītā: Twenty-fifth Anniversary Edition (State University of New York Press, 2009)</li><li>R.C. Zaehner, The Bhagavad Gītā (Oxford University Press, 1969)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="making">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Was Made</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ganesha"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ganesha.jpg" alt="Ganesha" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ganesha">Ganesha</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ganesha_Basohli_miniature_circa_1730_Dubost_p73.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">anonymous, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Indian tradition ascribes the Gita, like the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahabharata">Mahabharata</a>, to the sage <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vyasa">Vyasa</a>. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> legend narrates that Vyasa composed it and that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganesha">Ganesha</a> broke off one of his tusks and used it to write the epic down. Scholars consider Vyasa a mythical or symbolic author, in part because the name is also a title for the compiler of a text, and tradition makes him the compiler of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vedas">Vedas</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puranas">Puranas</a> too, texts dated about two millennia apart. Alexus McLeod holds it 'impossible to link the Bhagavad Gita to a single author', and Basham counts three or more authors or compilers, pointing to the discontinuous mixing of philosophical verses with passionately theistic ones.</p><p>J. A. B. van Buitenen finds the Gita so well knit into the Mahabharata that it was not an independent text that 'somehow wandered into the epic', but one conceived by the epic's authors to bring the dharmic dilemma of a war to a climax and solution. Basham cites chapter 5 as showing signs of interpolation: verses 5.23 to 5.28 give the sage's goal as the impersonal Brahman, while verse 5.29 gives it as the personal God <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a>.</p><p>The verses are mostly in the anushtubh metre, in which each shloka is a couplet of two quarters of eight syllables; at dramatic moments the text uses the tristubh metre of the Vedas, with eleven syllables to a quarter.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Bhagavad Gita&#8221; cites where it covers Dating, Authorship, Vāsudeva-Krishna roots:</p><ul><li>Kashi Nath Upadhyaya, Early Buddhism and the Bhagavadgītā (Motilal Banarsidass Publ, 1998)</li><li>Winthrop Sargeant, The Bhagavad Gītā: Twenty-fifth Anniversary Edition (State University of New York Press, 2009)</li><li>J.A.B. van Buitenen, The Bhagavadgita in the Mahabharata (University of Chicago Press, 2013)</li><li>Richard H. Davis, The 'Bhagavad Gita': A Biography (Princeton University Press, 2014)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="reception">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Reception</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/b-r-ambedkar"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/b-r-ambedkar.jpg" alt="B. R. Ambedkar" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/b-r-ambedkar">B. R. Ambedkar</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dr._Bhimrao_Ambedkar.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Commentary on the Gita is old and abundant: some 227 commentaries survive in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> alone, and Mysore Hiriyanna calls it 'one of the hardest books to interpret'. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adi-shankara">Adi Shankara</a>'s commentary (c. 800 CE), the oldest surviving and most influential, reads it as teaching the non-dualism of Atman and Brahman and treats bhakti as a practice that purifies the mind. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramanuja">Ramanuja</a>, in the 11th and early 12th centuries, read it as qualified monism (Vishishtadvaita) and identified the supreme Brahman with Narayana, or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madhvacharya">Madhvacharya</a> (c. 1250) held that the individual soul, Vishnu and the world are fundamentally different. Abhinavagupta wrote in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a> Shaiva tradition, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dnyaneshwar">Dnyaneshwar</a> wrote in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marathi">Marathi</a> in the 13th century. Chaitanya's followers used the Gita and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhagavata-purana">Bhagavata Purana</a> as the school's primary scriptural sources.</p><p>Catherine Robinson notes that scholars increasingly recognise the Gita's extraordinary prominence as a feature of modernity. Eric Sharpe dates the change to the 1880s and after 1900, Arvind Sharma to the 1920s. Gerald James Larson writes that if any one text comes near to embodying the totality of what it is to be a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a>, it is the Bhagavad Gita.</p><p>Readers disagree about it. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/b-r-ambedkar">B. R. Ambedkar</a> criticised its stance on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a> and called its defence of killing unethical, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Gandhi</a> credited his commitment to ahimsa (non-violence) to the Gita, and Lala Lajpat Rai and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bal-gangadhar-tilak">Tilak</a> read it as defending war when necessary.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Bhagavad Gita&#8221; cites where it covers Synthesis prioritizing dharma and bhakti, Vedanta, Classical Bhashya (commentaries), Adi Shankaracharya (c. 800 CE):</p><ul><li>Richard H. Davis, The 'Bhagavad Gita': A Biography (Princeton University Press, 2014)</li><li>Gavin Flood, An Introduction to Hinduism (Cambridge University Press, 1996)</li><li>Winthrop Sargeant, The Bhagavad Gītā: Twenty-fifth Anniversary Edition (State University of New York Press, 2009)</li><li>Edwin F. Bryant, Krishna: A Sourcebook (Oxford University Press, 2007)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="survival">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Survival &amp; Rediscovery</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/warren-hastings"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/warren-hastings.jpg" alt="Warren Hastings" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/warren-hastings">Warren Hastings</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Warren_Hastings_by_Sir_Joshua_Reynolds.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Sir Joshua Reynolds, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Gita is preserved within the Bhishma Parva of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahabharata">Mahabharata</a>, numbered as chapters 6.23 to 6.40 or 6.25 to 6.42 depending on the recension. It is also often kept and studied on its own, with its chapters renumbered 1 to 18. Its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> exist in numerous Indic scripts, among them the now dormant Sharada script. Unlike the large variations in the rest of the Mahabharata manuscripts, the Gita manuscripts show only minor variations, though alternate versions exist, such as one found in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a>, and Abhinavagupta commented on a slightly different recension from Shankara's. Gambhirananda suggests that old manuscripts may have had 745 verses, but no such manuscript has been found; Shankara stated that the Gita has 700, perhaps to prevent further insertions, and that figure has been the benchmark since. In April 2025 the Gita manuscript was added to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/memory-of-the-world-programme">Memory of the World Register</a>.</p><p>Persian translations were completed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a> in the sixteenth and seventeenth centuries. Charles Wilkins published the first English translation in 1785, with an introduction by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warren-hastings">Warren Hastings</a>, and French (1787), German and Russian versions followed. Richard Davis cites a count of 1,891 translations in 75 languages by 1982.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Bhagavad Gita&#8221; cites where it covers Manuscripts and layout, Narrative, Synthesis prioritizing dharma and bhakti, Vedanta:</p><ul><li>Richard H. Davis, The 'Bhagavad Gita': A Biography (Princeton University Press, 2014)</li><li>Kashi Nath Upadhyaya, Early Buddhism and the Bhagavadgītā (Motilal Banarsidass Publ, 1998)</li><li>Gavin Flood, An Introduction to Hinduism (Cambridge University Press, 1996)</li><li>Galvin Flood, The Bhagavad Gita: A New Translation (W.W. Norton &amp; Company, 2013)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi.jpg" alt="Mahatma Gandhi" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Mahatma Gandhi</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mahatma-Gandhi,_studio,_1931.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Elliott &amp; Fry, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In the modern age the Gita has served many causes. Neo-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindus</a> and Hindu nationalists took its emphasis on duty and action as a clue for their activism for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-independence">Indian independence</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bal-gangadhar-tilak">Tilak</a> wrote his commentary in jail in 1910 and 1911, and Vivekananda drew on the Gita for his lectures on the four yogas. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Gandhi</a>, who called it his 'spiritual dictionary', memorised the last 19 verses of chapter 2 and wrote a commentary in Gujarati in Yeravada jail in 1929. A. C. Bhaktivedanta Swami Prabhupada's Bhagavad-Gita as It Is is described as by far the most widely distributed English translation, and its publisher estimates sales at twenty-three million copies.</p><p>Readers have taken the battlefield as an allegory of the war within a person, as Vivekananda, Gandhi and Eknath Easwaran did, or as a real defence of the warrior's duty, as Steven Rosen, Laurie Patton and Stephen Mitchell do. It has also been criticised and defended on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a>, since <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a> tells <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arjuna">Arjuna</a> to follow his sva-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dharma">dharma</a>, the duty of his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varna">varna</a>, which Vivekananda read as the duty that comes with one's situation or profession.</p><p>Works of art drawn from it include Philip Glass's opera Satyagraha (1979), whose libretto consists of sayings from the Gita, and G. V. Iyer's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> film Bhagavad Gita (1993), which won the National Film Award for Best Film. J. Robert Oppenheimer recited a part of a verse from chapter 11 in a 1965 television documentary about the atomic bomb.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Bhagavad Gita&#8221; cites where it covers Modern prominence, Modern-era commentaries, The Gita in other European languages, Indian and Southeast Asian languages:</p><ul><li>Robert N. Minor, Modern Indian Interpreters of the Bhagavadgita (State University of New York, 1986)</li><li>Catherine A. Robinson, Interpretations of the Bhagavad-Gītā and Images of the Hindu Tradition: The Song of the Lord (Routledge, 2006)</li><li>J.A.B. van Buitenen, The Bhagavadgita in the Mahabharata (University of Chicago Press, 2013)</li><li>Winthrop Sargeant, The Bhagavad Gītā: Twenty-fifth Anniversary Edition (State University of New York Press, 2009)</li></ul></details>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Bhagavad Gita is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> scripture of 700 verses within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahabharata">Mahabharata</a>, a dialogue between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arjuna">Arjuna</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a> before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurukshetra">Kurukshetra</a> War. Tradition ascribes it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vyasa">Vyasa</a>; scholars see a composite work of the second or first century BCE, much commented on and translated.</em></p>

